# Level 21 with **only player two in the game**, and the movie that put the
# second player's side of the bubble tally on the board.
#
# `movies/level21-bubble.zmv` reaches the type `$04` object at (485,1340) --
# the bubble gun, weapon `$5E` -- and shoots the `$81:9B6B` creatures with it.
# Every one of those shots is Zeke's, so `enemy_9b6b_bubble`'s side arithmetic
# has one value to produce and three of that round's thirteen perturbations
# passed: forcing the score slot to the first player's, taking the side off
# bit 14 instead of bit 15, and answering `$05` alone where the ROM answers
# `$05` and `$06`. All three want the *other* player, and this is it.
#
# **It is a one-player game whose one player is player two.** The password is
# typed on port 1, exactly as every other password movie types it, and then the
# player-select window is answered on **port 2 alone**: no Start ever reaches
# port 1 after frame 1972, so Zeke never joins. `--pos` prints `--,--` in the
# first column for the whole run and the player it does print is the page whose
# `$64` holds `$1CEC`. That is a cheaper instrument than it looks -- with no
# player one on the board, *every* handler in the game that tests a player's
# collision id is offered `$06` instead of `$05`, and every shot that leaves the
# screen carries `$805E` instead of `$5E`.
#
# It also sidesteps the thing that makes an ordinary two-player movie on this
# level impossible. The camera tethers the two players to about 176 pixels of
# each other, and level 21's route north is a sequence of one-tile shafts: with
# both players walking it, one is stopped dead by the screen while the other
# climbs, and staggering them so both fit leaves whichever reaches the shaft
# mouth first stranded beside it. Three attempts died that way before this one. There is also exactly **one** type `$04` object on the
# level (`zamn_assets actors <rom> 22`), so two players could never both hold
# the weapon this movie is about.
#
# The route is `movies/level21-bubble.zmv`'s, with three frames moved, and each
# of the three is Julie standing thirty pixels east of where Zeke starts:
#
#   * **2704, not 2719.** The opening leg east is four frames from (288,2836)
#     rather than nineteen from (258,2836); both arrive at x=296, the mouth of
#     the first shaft. Every later leg keeps its original frame, because they
#     are anchored at walls rather than timed.
#   * **3985, not 4010.** The climb up the shaft at x=364 runs about 24 pixels
#     further in the same time -- the two traces are identical to the pixel as
#     far as y=1814 and then Zeke is held up by something Julie walks past -- so
#     the turn east comes 25 frames sooner. It is worth a frame of care: at 3990
#     the player ends at (463,1304) against a wall with the object uncollected,
#     and at 3985 it ends at (504,1338) with `$7E:1CF0` reading `$0040`.
#   * **`Down` held from 4790.** The tap-and-fire phase drifts *north* two
#     pixels a cycle from y=1338 and stops firing entirely above y≈1310, which
#     cost 35 of the 40 shots on the first attempt. `Down` held for the twenty
#     frames before the first tap seats the player on the floor at y≈1372, and
#     all forty land.
#
# What it buys: `d9b6b_bubble_slot_1` 15, the first time any input has credited
# a bubble to the second tally, with `d9b6b_bubble_slot_0` at zero; `f4ef_player`
# 30, every one of them the id `$06`; `score_slot_1` 2 against `score_slot_0` 0;
# `d845e_pass` 180. 136,648 calls checked, 0 diverged.
#
# Retune by watching the ammunition rather than the picture:
#   zamn_headless <rom> shot.png 6700 -m movies\level21-p2-bubble.zmv ^
#       --watch 1CF0,4700,6700,1 --pos 4790,6700,200
#
